  `result`, 0 `success` outcomes) and
  `::test_a_confirmation_landing_in_time_survives_a_late_adoption` (a
  `success` stamped at `9.0` `<` deadline `10.0`, adopted at `11.0`: it IS
  adopted as `success`). New cases beside them: a read provider's `success`
  stamped `> expires_at` and a `refused` stamped `== expires_at` are each
  replaced by the generic record (`timeout`, code `timed_out`, no `result`,
  the provider's refusal code absent); a provider that never returns is
  cancelled by the executor's timer at expiry and the call ends with the
  generic record whose message contains "timed out with emission" —
  `COUNTER_ACTION_TIMEOUTS` incremented — proving the timer is in force
  when no provider record arrives.
- AC41 (R10): `grep -rniE "adoption_lead|lead_seconds|deadline_margin|
  early_stop|deadline_lead"` over `modules/audio_output/`,
  `modules/audio_input/` and `modules/stream_control/` (code and
  manifests) matches 0 lines; the three settings schemas declare no
  lead/margin key; the gate review of `git diff <base>..HEAD -- modules/`
  finds no expression subtracting a literal or a setting from the call
  deadline **other than exactly two**, both in `modules/audio_input/`,
  both authorized here and each limited to its stated purpose: (i) R3's
  `capture_too_long` admission check — `seconds > (expiry − now) −
  grace_seconds`, evaluated once before any process is spawned — refuses
  a capture that could not finish before the deadline and moves nothing
  else: a recorder that was admitted is killed at `expiry` exactly
  (AC39); (ii) the optional transcription window of `audio.capture` —
  `(expiry − now) − 1 s`, measured once after the segment is stored —
  which exists for one reason only: the module must wait for the optional
  transcription before it can author the capture's `success` record
  carrying its `audio_ref`, and phase 1 adopts a `success` only when its
  completion stamp is strictly before `expires_at` (AC40), so that record
  must be stamped before `expiry`; the window never shortens the capture
  — the recorder's kill stays at `expiry` exactly, AC39 unchanged — it
  bounds the transcription request only (an exhausted window transcribes
  nothing, reports it and is still `success`, AC42), and it applies to no
  other action: not `audio.speak` or `audio.play`, not the scene or poll
  paths, whose stops, kills and reads happen at `expiry` itself. Any
  third subtraction, whatever its name — an adoption lead, a deadline
  margin, an early stop, a deadline lead — is a defect that the grep, the
  schema check and the diff review each reject; the README's playback and
  capture sections describe no lead; and AC38's `expiry − 0.05 s` success
  case passes.
- AC42 (R3, R10): The transcription window closes without losing the
  capture. With `transcription.enabled: true`, `timeout_seconds: 10`, the
  call deadline at `t = 100.0` on the injected clock (`expiry == 100.0`)
  and a transport that holds its answer, a segment stored at `t = 95.0`
  opens a window that closes at `t = 99.0` (`expiry − 1 s`, the
  transcription edge); when the optional transcription has not finished
  inside that window — the bound fires, or the module's wake from it is
  delayed — the terminal observation is `success` with its `audio_ref`
  (`size == 96 044`, the attachment still leased to the run),
  `transcription` absent and `transcription_status ==
  "failed:stt_timed_out"`, its `provider_completed_at` strictly `< 100.0`
  (`< expires_at`, adopted by the phase 1 in-time rule, never through
  R10), `action.completed` carrying `status: success`, exactly one record
  for the call, `COUNTER_ACTION_TIMEOUTS == 0` and 0 kills. Two tests
  drive it through the real executor: (a) the clock driven to the window
  edge — at `t = 98.99` the call is still running with exactly 1 request
  and 0 records; at `t = 99.0` the record above, stamped `== 99.0`; the
  transport's answer released afterwards at `t = 99.5` changes nothing
  (still one record, `failed:stt_timed_out`, no `transcription`); (b) the
  transcription finishes late — the clock advanced from `t = 98.0` to
  `t = 99.6` in one step, so the bound parked at `t = 99.0` wakes the
  module with the clock already at `99.6`, and the transport's answer is
  released at that same instant: whether or not the text is already in
  the module's hands when it wakes, the record is the one above, stamped
  `== 99.6 < 100.0`, adopted as `success`, the late text not adopted. The
  module decides on the clock it reads at its wake, not on which event
  woke it, and authors the record with no further wait. **What the
  module guarantees** (the two tests above): it never waits on anything
  scheduled at or after the window edge `expiry − 1 s`, so it authors
  and returns its observation at or before the edge whenever its wake
  comes on time, and a wake delayed past the edge by less than the
  reserve still returns `< expiry`. **Declared limit, not a guarantee:**
  the completion stamp is not the module's — `core/actions.py::_observe`
  takes it after the provider returns — so a wake stalled by at least
  the whole reserve (an event loop frozen for a second or more) lands at
  or after `expiry`, and the phase 1 rule then treats the record as a
  late `success` (not adopted; the generic timeout record replaces it).
  Its cause is the executor
  stamp, its bound is the reserve (a wake stalled by less than 1 s
  cannot reach `expiry`), and its only mitigation is the reserve itself;
  no test can pin it, and the README phase 2 limits clause (P20) records
  it beside the remote-readiness and executor-cancellation limits. A
  reader must not take this criterion as "the stamp is always before
  `expiry`": it is "the module returns at the edge at the latest, and a
  stall of the whole reserve is a documented residual". A window already
  exhausted when the transcription would start — a segment stored at
  `t = 99.0` or at `t = 99.5` (`(expiry − now) − 1 s ≤ 0`) — sends 0
  requests and reports `skipped:deadline`, still `success` with the
  `audio_ref`.

- AC43 (R2, R9, arbiter decision 22/09): No speech provider is assumed.
  With the shipped `config.yaml.example` and with every profile that
  enables the audio modules, the speech endpoint setting is EMPTY:
  `audio.speak` is not in the bound action set, the runtime's capability
  view names it with a value-free not-ready reason, no request is
  attempted and startup succeeds; the optional transcription endpoint is
  empty by the same rule and a capture without it is still `success` with
  its `audio_ref`. A chat-only profile behaves identically with the three
  phase 2 modules disabled. No shipped profile or example carries a
  localhost, vendor or model default; binding the action requires an
  operator-configured endpoint, and a test asserts the empty-setting
  startup with the action named as not ready and zero requests.

